> On Jun 4, 2019, at 7:13 AM, Ben Teaches English Overseas =
<ben@benteachesenglishoverseas.com> wrote:
>=20
> Hi, Chi!=20
>=20
> Welcome aboard! It's great to hear from you.=20
>=20
> You have a bachelor's degree, are in the process of getting your TEFL, =
and have a top-notch passport. These will go a long way abroad.=20
>=20
> Given that you rank money as a top priority, I would encourage you not =
to walk away from this contract... but to actually run away from it. =
This is very, very low. You can do much, much better.=20
>=20
> One of the first things I would do is consider how much of that $80k =
salary you had left over each month after you paid all of your expenses. =
Once you have this baseline, we can look at how much would be required =
to match it. It's probably a lot lower than you think.=20
>=20
> Matching a high salary in the countries you've specified is going to =
be rough. But it's entirely possible to do it somewhere like China.=20
>=20
> Generally speaking I encourage no one to ever work for less than 200 =
RMB or $30US per hour in China because it's just not necessary. I very =
rarely see training center contracts that pencil out. The best positions =
are in public and private K-12 and universities and colleges. Once =
you've worked your way through the manual, please let me know what =
you're thinking and we can go from there.=20
>=20
> Many people also find that they're not getting as many hits and as =
high quality of offers as they'd like because their resumes are off. You =
can upgrade your package if desired within the first 30 days and we can =
work on getting all of your documents looking professional and polished.=20=

>=20
> Ideally I'd like to see you get at very least double what this =
contract is offering in terms of hourly compensation. I look forward to =
continuing this conversation with you.=20
>=20
> Ben=20
>=20
